/**
 * The words that carry no subject, in nine languages.
 *
 * `keywords.j` works out what a page is about by weighting where a word appears
 * - the title, a heading, a code span - and the words that appear everywhere
 * defeat it: a German page produced `und, die, das, ist, der` and said nothing.
 * A stop list is the fix, and a stop list is per language, which is why this is
 * a module rather than a constant next to the scoring.
 *
 * There is no linguistics here. These are articles, pronouns, prepositions,
 * conjunctions, the auxiliary and modal verbs, and the handful of adverbs that
 * appear in every paragraph of every subject. A word that survives the list
 * still has to outscore the page's real subject to reach the tag, so the lists
 * are allowed to be incomplete; what they must not be is *wrong*, because a
 * subject word listed here can never appear on any page of any book in that
 * language.
 *
 * **English is always applied**, whatever the book's language. Technical writing
 * is bilingual wherever it quotes an identifier, and the literals below score
 * three points each because they sit in code spans. The book's own language is
 * added on top, chosen by `book.language` in `grimoire.toml`; `keywordStopwords`
 * adds to both.
 *
 * The nine are the eleven `src/locale.j` translates, less Japanese and Chinese.
 * Those two write without spaces between words, so a stop list cannot reach
 * them: separating a clause into words needs a segmenter, and `keywords.j` skips
 * those scripts instead of pretending a clause is a term.
 *
 * The lists are letters, which the repository's punctuation rule allows: a
 * Russian stop word is Cyrillic or it is not a Russian word, and German needs
 * its umlauts. The rest of that rule holds here as everywhere - no em dashes, no
 * curly quotes, no typographic ellipsis.
 *
 * Every list is sorted by codepoint, which is what `lists.sort` gives and what
 * `src/stopwords_test.j` checks: an addition lands in one place rather than
 * wherever the diff happened to be open. The accented forms trail the plain ones
 * as a consequence, so the German list ends in its umlauts.
 *
 * A word with an apostrophe in it is not worth listing. The term pattern in
 * `keywords.j` breaks on the apostrophe, so French `d'un` arrives as `d` and
 * `un`, both of which are already here on their own.
 * @module stopwords
 * @author mplx <jennifer@mplx.dev>
 * @license LGPL-3.0-only
 */
use strings;
use lists;

# The language tags this module has a list for, in the order `src/locale.j`
# names them. Kept as data so a test can walk it.
def const LANGUAGES as list of string init ["en", "de", "es", "fr", "it", "nl", "pl", "pt", "ru"];

# Not words in any language: the literals a configuration page is full of. They
# are here because a code span scores three, so a page documenting
# `enabled = false` would otherwise rank `false` above the setting it describes.
# Applied to every book, next to English.
def const LITERALS as list of string init ["true", "false", "null", "nil", "none"];

